---
import TrialChart from "../components/TrialChart.astro";
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
---

<Base
	title="Benchmarks and trials — Bunpō"
	description="Predicted loads, repeated measured runs, and real production CI trials for Bunpō."
>
	<span class="eyebrow">benchmarks &amp; trials</span>
	<h1>Measured numbers, including the cases that will not balance.</h1>
	<p class="lead">
		Predicted load balance and actual CI wall time are two different numbers. Setup costs,
		indivisible slow files, runner hardware differences, and queue delays all show up in real
		runs. Both are reported separately below.
	</p>

	<section class="band" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
		<div class="band-head">
			<div class="band-label">
				<strong>Before &amp; after</strong>
				<span>Switch suites</span>
			</div>
			<div>
				<h2>Interactive before-and-after comparison</h2>
				<TrialChart />
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="band">
		<div class="band-head">
			<div class="band-label">
				<strong>Live CI trial</strong>
				<span>LaTabdhir · GitHub Actions</span>
			</div>
			<div>
				<h2>Playwright E2E on two self-hosted runners (v0.0.5)</h2>
				<p>
					An outside maintainer and their coding agents wired <code>nuggocto/bunpo@v0</code> into{" "}
					<code>.github/workflows/e2e.yml</code> from the Playwright guide alone: 68 specs,
					232 cases, Docker Compose per runner, across one x64 runner and one ARM64 runner.
				</p>
				<div class="table-wrap">
					<table>
						<thead>
							<tr>
								<th>Run</th>
								<th>Slowest browser job</th>
								<th>Verify + merge</th>
								<th>Workflow elapsed (incl. queue)</th>
							</tr>
						</thead>
						<tbody>
							<tr>
								<td>Single machine (ARM64 baseline)</td>
								<td>19m 12s</td>
								<td>—</td>
								<td>19m 47s</td>
							</tr>
							<tr>
								<td>Cold (2 machines, by count)</td>
								<td>8m 46s</td>
								<td>17s</td>
								<td>9m 58s</td>
							</tr>
							<tr>
								<td>Warm (2 machines, timed 4m42s / 4m42s)</td>
								<td>7m 32s</td>
								<td>16s</td>
								<td>17m 49s (13m 09s x64 queue delay)</td>
							</tr>
						</tbody>
					</table>
				</div>
				<p class="small muted">
					The cold run had no timings, so its drop from 19m 47s comes from adding a second,
					faster machine. In the warm run, Bunpō predicted 4m42s on both shards; actual Playwright wall was
					3m 32s on x64 vs 6m 29s on ARM64, and another CI job queued x64 for 13m 09s. Static
					file-level splitting assumes similar machines starting together.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="band">
		<div class="band-head">
			<div class="band-label">
				<strong>Local trials</strong>
				<span>7 real suites · 4 CPUs each</span>
			</div>
			<div>
				<h2>Production suites on LaTabdhir and Majlisna</h2>
				<p>
					Each machine ran alone pinned with <code>taskset</code> to 4 CPUs to eliminate
					host contention, measuring test-command wall time (excluding CI checkout and
					dependency install). Every round ran the exact same test count as baseline with{" "}
					<code>verify</code> and <code>merge</code> exiting 0.
				</p>
				<p>
					Bunpō's own effect is the <em>Timed vs count</em> column. It is large when a few
					slow files dominate and small when files are already even. On LaTabdhir's SQLite
					suite, an existing <code>crc32</code> hash of test IDs was about as fast as Bunpō
					(~59s after rerunning two
					noisy shards).
				</p>
				<div class="table-wrap">
					<table>
						<thead>
							<tr>
								<th>Suite</th>
								<th>Runner</th>
								<th>1 machine</th>
								<th>4 machines (count)</th>
								<th>4 machines (timed R2 / R3)</th>
								<th>Timed vs count</th>
								<th>Timed vs 1 machine</th>
							</tr>
						</thead>
						<tbody>
							<tr>
								<td>LaTabdhir Postgres parity</td>
								<td>pytest (495 files)</td>
								<td>25m 30s</td>
								<td>6m 36s (54.5s spread)</td>
								<td>6m 16s / 6m 19s (5.4s / 10.3s)</td>
								<td>4–5% less</td>
								<td>4.0x</td>
							</tr>
							<tr>
								<td>LaTabdhir backend SQLite</td>
								<td>pytest -n 4 (495 files)</td>
								<td>3m 42s</td>
								<td>1m 27s (20.3s spread)</td>
								<td>1m 01s / 1m 03s (2.6s / 5.7s)</td>
								<td>27–30% less</td>
								<td>3.6x</td>
							</tr>
							<tr>
								<td>LaTabdhir front</td>
								<td>Vitest full-cost (294 files)</td>
								<td>2m 03s</td>
								<td>37.6s (5.8s spread)</td>
								<td>34.3s / 36.6s (1.7s / 2.4s)</td>
								<td>3–9% less, none vs a count rerun (33.7s)</td>
								<td>3.4x</td>
							</tr>
							<tr>
								<td>LaTabdhir E2E</td>
								<td>Playwright workers: 1 (68 specs)</td>
								<td>5m 09s</td>
								<td>3m 09s (113.9s spread, 1 flake)</td>
								<td>1m 34s–1m 43s clean (2m 45s / 2m 24s w/ flake)</td>
								<td>not comparable (flaky retries)</td>
								<td>~3.0x clean</td>
							</tr>
							<tr>
								<td>Majlisna backend Postgres</td>
								<td>pytest (113 files)</td>
								<td>8m 10s</td>
								<td>4m 15s (179.5s spread)</td>
								<td>2m 08s / 2m 13s (7.3s / 11.1s)</td>
								<td>48–50% less</td>
								<td>3.8x</td>
							</tr>
							<tr>
								<td>Majlisna E2E (17 projects)</td>
								<td>Playwright (70 specs)</td>
								<td>6m 14s</td>
								<td>3m 28s (140.6s spread)</td>
								<td>3m 28s (floored by 190s WS spec)</td>
								<td>none</td>
								<td>1.8x</td>
							</tr>
							<tr>
								<td>Majlisna front</td>
								<td>Vitest full-cost (151 files)</td>
								<td>24.7s</td>
								<td>7.8s (1.08s spread)</td>
								<td>7.8s / 7.7s (0.56s / 0.32s)</td>
								<td>none</td>
								<td>3.2x</td>
							</tr>
						</tbody>
					</table>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="band">
		<div class="band-head">
			<div class="band-label">
				<strong>Synthetic suite</strong>
				<span>docs/benchmark.md</span>
			</div>
			<div>
				<h2>Declared benchmark, tight split, and indivisible file</h2>
				<p>
					Run with real pytest processes on 4 parallel machines (fixed pytest startup: 134 ms
					per machine). The declared target is predicted slowest / fastest ≤ 1.10 on the
					Many files suite.
				</p>
				<div class="table-wrap">
					<table>
						<thead>
							<tr>
								<th>Suite</th>
								<th>Shape</th>
								<th>Predicted ratio (R3)</th>
								<th>Wall ratio (R3)</th>
								<th>Slowest wall (R3)</th>
							</tr>
						</thead>
						<tbody>
							<tr>
								<td>Many files</td>
								<td>40 files, 100–900 ms</td>
								<td>1.021</td>
								<td>1.028</td>
								<td>5,042 ms</td>
							</tr>
							<tr>
								<td>Tight</td>
								<td>13 files with exact 3s optimal split</td>
								<td>1.104</td>
								<td>1.104</td>
								<td>3,392 ms</td>
							</tr>
							<tr>
								<td>Indivisible</td>
								<td>One 6,000 ms file + twelve 500 ms files</td>
								<td>2.995</td>
								<td>2.839</td>
								<td>6,174 ms</td>
							</tr>
						</tbody>
					</table>
				</div>
				<p class="small muted">
					Longest-first dealing guarantees a makespan within 4/3 of optimal, not an exact
					partition search: the Tight suite shows the gap, and Indivisible shows why{" "}
					<code>bunpo split</code> warns when one file exceeds the average machine load.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="band">
		<div class="band-head">
			<div class="band-label">
				<strong>Overhead &amp; limits</strong>
				<span>Binary, split speed, bounds</span>
			</div>
			<div>
				<h2>31 ms to split 50,000 tests across 256 machines</h2>
				<p>
					Bunpō runs on every machine on every build, so startup, split speed, and binary
					size stay small. Measured on the Go benchmark suite, the <code>v0.0.6</code>{" "}
					release binaries, and the live GitHub Actions trial:
				</p>
				<div class="table-wrap">
					<table>
						<thead>
							<tr>
								<th>Metric</th>
								<th>Workload / scope</th>
								<th>Measured value</th>
							</tr>
						</thead>
						<tbody>
							<tr>
								<td><code>BenchmarkSplit</code></td>
								<td>50,000 tests, 256 machines (budget: &lt; 1 s)</td>
								<td>~31 ms (403 allocs)</td>
							</tr>
							<tr>
								<td><code>verify</code> + <code>merge</code> CI job</td>
								<td>Live GitHub Actions (LaTabdhir, 68 specs, incl. checkout &amp; artifacts)</td>
								<td>16–17 s total</td>
							</tr>
							<tr>
								<td>Release binary size (<code>v0.0.6</code>)</td>
								<td>Static binary, Go stdlib only (<code>linux/amd64</code>)</td>
								<td>3.2 MiB (3.0–3.3 MiB across targets)</td>
							</tr>
							<tr>
								<td>Input &amp; report caps</td>
								<td><code>timings.json</code>, manifests, JUnit XML, stdin list</td>
								<td>64 MiB per file · 32 XML depth · 2M tokens</td>
							</tr>
						</tbody>
					</table>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>
</Base>
